        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Early in the spaceflight program, it was recognized that the cost and frequency of spaceflights made it mandatory that maximum use of each opportunity be made.  Since power, weight, volume and crew time limitations guaranteed that all portions of biological specimens could not be utilized by the primary Principle Investigators on any one spaceflight, the concept of a Biospecimen Sharing Program was developed.  This Program had two aspects: post-flight analysis of unused biospecimens by “secondary” Investigators whose experiments were compatible with the treatments imposed by the primary investigators; and archiving/storage of unused biospecimens for future experiment proposals or re-analysis requirements resulting from new/”modern” procedures.<br><br>In order that the data and results from this program be tracked and archived, an artificial payload, “Biospecimen Sharing Program” (BSP), was created. Biospecimen, Document, Configuration, Hardware, Parameters, Inventory, Session,  and Research Subject all depend on the source of the biospecimen, so that information will be obtained from records associated with the source by accessing data through the experiment records listed under this payload.<br>
        </div>

